@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Authorization
@* Exercise 081 - Cascading Auth State Basics (advanced).
   Goal:   Show different markup to a signed-in user, a signed-out one, and one
           whose state has not arrived yet.
   Drills: <AuthorizeView> and its three fragments, the ClaimsPrincipal it hands to
           Authorized as @context, and gating on a role.
   Passes: dotnet test --filter FullyQualifiedName~Ex081_ *@

@* TODO: render two <AuthorizeView> blocks.
   The first has all three fragments:
     Authorizing   <span id="pending">checking…</span>
     Authorized    <span id="user">…</span> holding the user's name, from the
                   ClaimsPrincipal the fragment hands you as @context.User.
     NotAuthorized <span id="anon">sign in</span>
   The second gates on a role rather than on being signed in at all, and has only an
   Authorized fragment:
     <span id="admin">admin tools</span> for members of the "admin" role.
   Authorizing is not a decoration: an AuthenticationStateProvider hands back a Task,
   and until it completes there is genuinely no answer to show. *@

